<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.4 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-three-dimensional-geometry" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Three Dimensional Geometry</title>

  <!-- Scope (from the curriculum design): Solids to be used: cubes, cuboids, triangular-based prisms, and triangular-, rectangular- and square-based pyramids. Composite solids are not used in this sub-strand. -->

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In earlier grades you used Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios to find lengths and angles in flat shapes, and you worked out surface areas and volumes of solids. Here you will use the same tools inside solids.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will work with cubes, cuboids, prisms and pyramids. You will identify skew lines and the projection of a line onto a plane, and calculate the lengths of lines in three dimensions. You will then find the angle between skew lines, between two lines, between a line and a plane, and between two planes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key skill is to pick out the right-angled triangle inside the solid. Builders, carpenters and engineers do this when they design roof trusses, staircases, water tanks and bridges.
    </p>
  </introduction>

